WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:16.962
This is the Tamil Learner Corpus. It turns students' handwritten Tamil into a collection that teachers and researchers can search and learn from. This short video shows two things. First, how a script is added, and its errors marked. Second, the insights the collection gives.

2
00:00:20.162 --> 00:00:32.438
Start by uploading the scanned script, and choose the level, the year, and the type of writing. The record number is filled in for you, and no student names are needed anywhere. Then click Process script.

3
00:00:32.738 --> 00:00:42.689
The platform then reads the handwriting. This may take a few minutes, depending on the pages uploaded. When it is ready, open the processed page to review it.

4
00:00:42.989 --> 00:00:53.857
Next, check the text that was detected. The scan is on the left, the detected text is in the middle, and on the right is a list of the words the platform is not sure about.

5
00:00:54.157 --> 00:01:05.430
For each review item, pick the word that matches the original scanned document, or type it in. Keep the student's own mistakes exactly as written. Those are what we want to study.

6
00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:17.110
If the text detection did not work in some parts, because of the scan quality or the handwriting, you can type the text in directly instead. All edits are saved automatically.

7
00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:32.182
After checking the words, continue to the next step: marking errors. The platform first looks for errors by itself. Each possible error becomes a card, with a suggested correction and an error type. The colours show the four groups of errors.

8
00:01:32.482 --> 00:01:47.212
These are the error types, named in Tamil, in four groups: letters and spelling, word forms, grammar, and sentences and punctuation. Every error, whether found by the platform or added by a teacher, is tagged from this same list.

9
00:01:47.512 --> 00:01:58.828
For example, this card shows a mix-up between two similar-sounding Tamil letters. It suggests the correct word, and tags the error with its type, from the letters and spelling group.

10
00:01:59.128 --> 00:02:07.969
You stay in control. For each card, you can accept it, change the correction, or mark it as not an error. Nothing is changed without you.

11
00:02:08.269 --> 00:02:16.683
If the platform missed something, select the words and add the error yourself. You can also click between two words to add a missing word.

12
00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:22.945
When all the cards are done, the corrected text shows the student's writing, with every error marked.

13
00:02:23.245 --> 00:02:32.278
Last, check the summary and save the record. It joins the corpus straight away. You can also stop half way, and come back later to finish.

14
00:02:35.478 --> 00:02:42.976
Every saved script appears on the dashboard straight away, with word counts, levels, and the most common error types.

15
00:02:43.276 --> 00:02:52.416
In Analyze, you can search the whole collection. The error list shows every example of one error type in its sentence, and it can be downloaded.

16
00:02:52.716 --> 00:03:06.314
Insights is where the corpus is turned into findings. One script is not enough for that. So for now, these pages run on simulated data, made up to show how the analysis would look, and clearly marked as such.

17
00:03:06.614 --> 00:03:16.885
As more scripts are added, the platform can pick up on patterns and trends. For example, which errors students grow out of as they move up the levels, and which ones stay.

18
00:03:17.185 --> 00:03:22.400
And with enough scripts at each level, it can point to what to focus on at each level.

19
00:03:22.699 --> 00:03:34.062
From a handwritten page, to evidence for teaching, with a teacher checking every step. You can try the platform yourself, at the link on screen. No sign-in is needed.
